course: dictionary
lesson: 5485
title: Later: after some time
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Later has two main jobs. It can mean after some time, not now: «I will do it later.» It can also mean after something else happened: «Two hours later, the tank was full.» In both, the word points forward in time, so you read on to find out what came next.
explain: Check the clues around it. In «Going east, the clock usually moves one hour later in each new time zone», later means the time is ahead of where you started. Try your meaning in the sentence: "one hour ahead" fits, so you know you are right.
explain: As an adjective, later means coming after another one: a later bus, a later chapter. Notice the family of words: late is the starting point, later is further on, and latest is the furthest of all.
fact: Later means after some time, not now.
fact: Later can also mean after something else happened, as in «Two hours later, the tank was full.»
fact: «Five days later» means you add five days to the starting day, so the story moves forward.
fact: As an adjective, later means coming after another one: a later bus.
fact: The family is late, later, latest.
exercise: «I will do it later.» What does later mean here? | Earlier than now | *After some time, not now | Very quickly | Never at all
exercise: Halloween, 31 October, is a Saturday. Bonfire Night, 5 November, is 5 days later. What day is Bonfire Night? | Sunday | Friday | Saturday | *Thursday
exercise: In «a later chapter», what does later mean? | *Coming after another one | Coming before | Very quiet | Sad
test: «Two hours later, the tank was full.» Later means: | Never | *After the start | Before the start | At the same time
test: Which words are in the same family as late? | *late, later, latest | lend, later, lean | later, lunch, latch | late, lazy, lamp
exam: Which meaning fits «I will call you later»? | Whenever you like, never | Before breakfast | Quite loudly | *After some time, not now
